1
00:00:03,519 --> 00:00:08,917
If that staid old house near the Green at Richmond should ever come to be haunted when I am dead,

2
00:00:08,917 --> 00:00:14,680
it will be haunted, surely, by my ghost. O the many, many nights and days through which

3
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:20,358
the unquiet spirit within me haunted that house when Estella lived there! Let my body be where

4
00:00:20,358 --> 00:00:26,597
it would, my spirit was always wandering, wandering, wandering, about that house.

5
00:00:26,597 --> 00:00:31,957
The lady with whom Estella was placed, Mrs. Brandley by name, was a widow,

6
00:00:31,957 --> 00:00:37,358
with one daughter several years older than Estella. The mother looked young,

7
00:00:37,358 --> 00:00:42,597
and the daughter looked old; the mother’s complexion was pink, and the daughter’s was

8
00:00:42,597 --> 00:00:50,319
yellow; the mother set up for frivolity, and the daughter for theology. They were in what

9
00:00:50,319 --> 00:00:56,358
is called a good position, and visited, and were visited by, numbers of people. Little,

10
00:00:56,358 --> 00:01:01,917
if any, community of feeling subsisted between them and Estella, but the understanding was

11
00:01:01,917 --> 00:01:07,641
established that they were necessary to her, and that she was necessary to them.

12
00:01:08,199 --> 00:01:13,839
Mrs. Brandley had been a friend of Miss Havisham’s before the time of her seclusion.

13
00:01:13,839 --> 00:01:19,519
In Mrs. Brandley’s house and out of Mrs. Brandley’s house, I suffered every kind and

14
00:01:19,519 --> 00:01:26,237
degree of torture that Estella could cause me. The nature of my relations with her,

15
00:01:26,237 --> 00:01:32,160
which placed me on terms of familiarity without placing me on terms of favour, conduced to my

16
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:39,639
distraction. She made use of me to tease other admirers, and she turned the very familiarity

17
00:01:39,641 --> 00:01:46,641
between herself and me to the account of putting a constant slight on my devotion to her. If I had

18
00:01:46,641 --> 00:01:53,480
been her secretary, steward, half-brother, poor relation,—if I had been a younger brother of her

19
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:58,199
appointed husband,—I could not have seemed to myself further from my hopes when I was

20
00:01:58,199 --> 00:02:06,116
nearest to her. The privilege of calling her by her name and hearing her call me by mine became,

21
00:02:06,116 --> 00:02:12,199
under the circumstances an aggravation of my trials; and while I think it likely that it

22
00:02:12,199 --> 00:02:18,480
almost maddened her other lovers, I know too certainly that it almost maddened me.

23
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:23,877
She had admirers without end. No doubt my jealousy made an admirer of every

24
00:02:23,879 --> 00:02:29,519
one who went near her; but there were more than enough of them without that.

25
00:02:29,519 --> 00:02:35,237
I saw her often at Richmond, I heard of her often in town, and I used often to take her

26
00:02:35,237 --> 00:02:41,879
and the Brandleys on the water; there were picnics, fête days, plays, operas, concerts,

27
00:02:41,879 --> 00:02:47,559
parties, all sorts of pleasures, through which I pursued her,—and they were all miseries to

28
00:02:47,559 --> 00:02:53,199
me. I never had one hour’s happiness in her society, and yet my mind all round

29
00:02:53,199 --> 00:02:58,758
the four-and-twenty hours was harping on the happiness of having her with me unto death.

30
00:02:58,758 --> 00:03:03,116
Throughout this part of our intercourse,—and it lasted, as will presently be seen,

31
00:03:03,116 --> 00:03:07,800
for what I then thought a long time,—she habitually reverted to that tone which

32
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:13,480
expressed that our association was forced upon us. There were other times when she would come

33
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:20,000
to a sudden check in this tone and in all her many tones, and would seem to pity me.

34
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,718
“Pip, Pip,” she said one evening, coming to such a check, when we

35
00:03:23,718 --> 00:03:30,438
sat apart at a darkening window of the house in Richmond; “will you never take warning?”

36
00:03:30,438 --> 00:03:31,957
“Of what?”

37
00:03:31,957 --> 00:03:33,917
“Of me.”

38
00:03:33,917 --> 00:03:38,000
“Warning not to be attracted by you, do you mean, Estella?”

39
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:43,116
“Do I mean! If you don’t know what I mean, you are blind.”

40
00:03:43,116 --> 00:03:48,038
I should have replied that Love was commonly reputed blind, but for the reason that I always

41
00:03:48,038 --> 00:03:53,680
was restrained—and this was not the least of my miseries—by a feeling that it was ungenerous

42
00:03:53,680 --> 00:03:59,358
to press myself upon her, when she knew that she could not choose but obey Miss Havisham.

43
00:03:59,358 --> 00:04:05,436
My dread always was, that this knowledge on her part laid me under a heavy disadvantage

44
00:04:05,438 --> 00:04:12,078
with her pride, and made me the subject of a rebellious struggle in her bosom.

45
00:04:12,078 --> 00:04:16,000
“At any rate,” said I, “I have no warning given me just now,

46
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,160
for you wrote to me to come to you, this time.”

47
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:25,277
“That’s true,” said Estella, with a cold careless smile that always chilled me.

48
00:04:25,277 --> 00:04:30,398
After looking at the twilight without, for a little while, she went on to say:—

49
00:04:30,398 --> 00:04:33,160
“The time has come round when Miss Havisham wishes to have

50
00:04:33,160 --> 00:04:37,639
me for a day at Satis. You are to take me there, and bring me back,

51
00:04:37,641 --> 00:04:43,160
if you will. She would rather I did not travel alone, and objects to receiving my maid,

52
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:48,718
for she has a sensitive horror of being talked of by such people. Can you take me?”

53
00:04:48,718 --> 00:04:50,758
“Can I take you, Estella!”

54
00:04:50,758 --> 00:04:54,398
“You can then? The day after to-morrow, if you please. You

55
00:04:54,398 --> 00:04:59,116
are to pay all charges out of my purse. You hear the condition of your going?”

56
00:04:59,116 --> 00:05:02,680
“And must obey,” said I.

57
00:05:02,680 --> 00:05:08,237
This was all the preparation I received for that visit, or for others like it; Miss Havisham never

58
00:05:08,237 --> 00:05:15,199
wrote to me, nor had I ever so much as seen her handwriting. We went down on the next day but one,

59
00:05:15,199 --> 00:05:19,237
and we found her in the room where I had first beheld her, and it is needless to

60
00:05:19,237 --> 00:05:25,436
add that there was no change in Satis House. She was even more dreadfully fond of Estella

61
00:05:25,438 --> 00:05:31,758
than she had been when I last saw them together; I repeat the word advisedly, for there was something

62
00:05:31,758 --> 00:05:37,321
positively dreadful in the energy of her looks and embraces. She hung upon Estella’s beauty,

63
00:05:37,879 --> 00:05:42,800
hung upon her words, hung upon her gestures, and sat mumbling her own trembling fingers

64
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:48,038
while she looked at her, as though she were devouring the beautiful creature she had reared.

65
00:05:48,038 --> 00:05:52,758
From Estella she looked at me, with a searching glance that seemed to pry into my heart and

66
00:05:52,758 --> 00:06:00,358
probe its wounds. “How does she use you, Pip; how does she use you?” she asked me again,

67
00:06:00,358 --> 00:06:05,436
with her witch-like eagerness, even in Estella’s hearing. But,

68
00:06:05,438 --> 00:06:11,199
when we sat by her flickering fire at night, she was most weird; for then,

69
00:06:11,199 --> 00:06:16,957
keeping Estella’s hand drawn through her arm and clutched in her own hand, she extorted from her,

70
00:06:16,957 --> 00:06:21,917
by dint of referring back to what Estella had told her in her regular letters, the names and

71
00:06:21,917 --> 00:06:27,839
conditions of the men whom she had fascinated; and as Miss Havisham dwelt upon this roll,

72
00:06:27,839 --> 00:06:33,116
with the intensity of a mind mortally hurt and diseased, she sat with her other hand on

73
00:06:33,116 --> 00:06:40,398
her crutch stick, and her chin on that, and her wan bright eyes glaring at me, a very spectre.

74
00:06:40,398 --> 00:06:44,480
I saw in this, wretched though it made me, and bitter the sense of dependence

75
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:49,718
and even of degradation that it awakened,—I saw in this that Estella was set to wreak

76
00:06:49,718 --> 00:06:54,078
Miss Havisham’s revenge on men, and that she was not to be given to me until she

77
00:06:54,078 --> 00:06:59,237
had gratified it for a term. I saw in this, a reason for her being beforehand

78
00:06:59,237 --> 00:07:04,718
assigned to me. Sending her out to attract and torment and do mischief, Miss Havisham

79
00:07:04,718 --> 00:07:09,438
sent her with the malicious assurance that she was beyond the reach of all admirers,

80
00:07:09,438 --> 00:07:15,199
and that all who staked upon that cast were secured to lose. I saw in this that I, too,

81
00:07:15,199 --> 00:07:21,879
was tormented by a perversion of ingenuity, even while the prize was reserved for me. I saw in this

82
00:07:21,879 --> 00:07:27,319
the reason for my being staved off so long and the reason for my late guardian’s declining to commit

83
00:07:27,319 --> 00:07:34,319
himself to the formal knowledge of such a scheme. In a word, I saw in this Miss Havisham as I had

84
00:07:34,319 --> 00:07:41,116
her then and there before my eyes, and always had had her before my eyes; and I saw in this,

85
00:07:41,116 --> 00:07:47,597
the distinct shadow of the darkened and unhealthy house in which her life was hidden from the sun.

86
00:07:47,597 --> 00:07:52,957
The candles that lighted that room of hers were placed in sconces on the wall. They were high

87
00:07:52,957 --> 00:07:58,641
from the ground, and they burnt with the steady dulness of artificial light in air that is seldom

88
00:07:58,641 --> 00:08:05,758
renewed. As I looked round at them, and at the pale gloom they made, and at the stopped clock,

89
00:08:05,758 --> 00:08:10,597
and at the withered articles of bridal dress upon the table and the ground, and at her own

90
00:08:10,597 --> 00:08:16,398
awful figure with its ghostly reflection thrown large by the fire upon the ceiling and the wall,

91
00:08:16,398 --> 00:08:21,718
I saw in everything the construction that my mind had come to, repeated and thrown back

92
00:08:21,718 --> 00:08:28,116
to me. My thoughts passed into the great room across the landing where the table was spread,

93
00:08:28,116 --> 00:08:33,277
and I saw it written, as it were, in the falls of the cobwebs from the centre-piece,

94
00:08:33,277 --> 00:08:39,116
in the crawlings of the spiders on the cloth, in the tracks of the mice as they betook their little

95
00:08:39,116 --> 00:08:45,480
quickened hearts behind the panels, and in the gropings and pausings of the beetles on the floor.

96
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:50,000
It happened on the occasion of this visit that some sharp words arose between Estella

97
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:55,038
and Miss Havisham. It was the first time I had ever seen them opposed.

98
00:08:55,038 --> 00:09:00,917
We were seated by the fire, as just now described, and Miss Havisham still had Estella’s

99
00:09:00,917 --> 00:09:07,559
arm drawn through her own, and still clutched Estella’s hand in hers, when Estella gradually

100
00:09:07,559 --> 00:09:13,358
began to detach herself. She had shown a proud impatience more than once before,

101
00:09:13,358 --> 00:09:18,718
and had rather endured that fierce affection than accepted or returned it.

102
00:09:18,718 --> 00:09:24,398
“What!” said Miss Havisham, flashing her eyes upon her, “are you tired of me?”

103
00:09:24,398 --> 00:09:28,838
“Only a little tired of myself,” replied Estella, disengaging her arm,

104
00:09:28,839 --> 00:09:33,800
and moving to the great chimney-piece, where she stood looking down at the fire.

105
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:37,319
“Speak the truth, you ingrate!” cried Miss Havisham,

106
00:09:37,319 --> 00:09:43,116
passionately striking her stick upon the floor; “you are tired of me.”

107
00:09:43,116 --> 00:09:45,800
Estella looked at her with perfect composure,

108
00:09:45,800 --> 00:09:50,438
and again looked down at the fire. Her graceful figure and her beautiful face

109
00:09:50,438 --> 00:09:56,917
expressed a self-possessed indifference to the wild heat of the other, that was almost cruel.

110
00:09:56,917 --> 00:10:04,160
“You stock and stone!” exclaimed Miss Havisham. “You cold, cold heart!”

111
00:10:04,160 --> 00:10:08,957
“What?” said Estella, preserving her attitude of indifference as she leaned against the great

112
00:10:08,957 --> 00:10:16,000
chimney-piece and only moving her eyes; “do you reproach me for being cold? You?”

113
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:19,160
“Are you not?” was the fierce retort.

114
00:10:19,160 --> 00:10:24,957
“You should know,” said Estella. “I am what you have made me. Take all the praise,

115
00:10:24,957 --> 00:10:33,480
take all the blame; take all the success, take all the failure; in short, take me.”

116
00:10:33,480 --> 00:10:37,519
“O, look at her, look at her!” cried Miss Havisham, bitterly; “Look at her so hard and

117
00:10:37,519 --> 00:10:42,000
thankless, on the hearth where she was reared! Where I took her into this wretched breast when

118
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:47,879
it was first bleeding from its stabs, and where I have lavished years of tenderness upon her!”

119
00:10:47,879 --> 00:10:52,879
“At least I was no party to the compact,” said Estella, “for if I could walk and speak,

120
00:10:52,879 --> 00:10:58,160
when it was made, it was as much as I could do. But what would you have?

121
00:10:58,160 --> 00:11:03,800
You have been very good to me, and I owe everything to you. What would you have?”

122
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:05,758
“Love,” replied the other.

123
00:11:05,758 --> 00:11:07,358
“You have it.”

124
00:11:07,358 --> 00:11:10,436
“I have not,” said Miss Havisham.

125
00:11:10,438 --> 00:11:16,597
“Mother by adoption,” retorted Estella, never departing from the easy grace of her attitude,

126
00:11:16,597 --> 00:11:23,237
never raising her voice as the other did, never yielding either to anger or tenderness,—“mother

127
00:11:23,237 --> 00:11:31,116
by adoption, I have said that I owe everything to you. All I possess is freely yours. All that

128
00:11:31,116 --> 00:11:37,718
you have given me, is at your command to have again. Beyond that, I have nothing. And if you

129
00:11:37,718 --> 00:11:45,758
ask me to give you, what you never gave me, my gratitude and duty cannot do impossibilities.”

130
00:11:45,758 --> 00:11:50,319
“Did I never give her love!” cried Miss Havisham, turning wildly to me.

131
00:11:50,319 --> 00:11:54,678
“Did I never give her a burning love, inseparable from jealousy at all times,

132
00:11:54,680 --> 00:12:01,879
and from sharp pain, while she speaks thus to me! Let her call me mad, let her call me mad!”

133
00:12:01,879 --> 00:12:07,038
“Why should I call you mad,” returned Estella, “I, of all people? Does any

134
00:12:07,038 --> 00:12:13,398
one live, who knows what set purposes you have, half as well as I do? Does any one live,

135
00:12:13,398 --> 00:12:18,319
who knows what a steady memory you have, half as well as I do? I who have sat on

136
00:12:18,319 --> 00:12:23,078
this same hearth on the little stool that is even now beside you there, learning your

137
00:12:23,078 --> 00:12:28,718
lessons and looking up into your face, when your face was strange and frightened me!”

138
00:12:28,718 --> 00:12:34,237
“Soon forgotten!” moaned Miss Havisham. “Times soon forgotten!”

139
00:12:34,237 --> 00:12:37,480
“No, not forgotten,” retorted Estella,—“not

140
00:12:37,480 --> 00:12:42,718
forgotten, but treasured up in my memory. When have you found me false to your teaching? When

141
00:12:42,718 --> 00:12:47,680
have you found me unmindful of your lessons? When have you found me giving admission here,”

142
00:12:47,680 --> 00:12:54,641
she touched her bosom with her hand, “to anything that you excluded? Be just to me.”

143
00:12:54,641 --> 00:13:01,718
“So proud, so proud!” moaned Miss Havisham, pushing away her grey hair with both her hands.

144
00:13:01,718 --> 00:13:08,319
“Who taught me to be proud?” returned Estella. “Who praised me when I learnt my lesson?”

145
00:13:08,319 --> 00:13:14,038
“So hard, so hard!” moaned Miss Havisham, with her former action.

146
00:13:14,038 --> 00:13:20,641
“Who taught me to be hard?” returned Estella. “Who praised me when I learnt my lesson?”

147
00:13:20,641 --> 00:13:25,199
“But to be proud and hard to me!” Miss Havisham quite shrieked,

148
00:13:25,199 --> 00:13:33,718
as she stretched out her arms. “Estella, Estella, Estella, to be proud and hard to me!”

149
00:13:33,718 --> 00:13:36,839
Estella looked at her for a moment with a kind of calm wonder,

150
00:13:37,641 --> 00:13:44,879
but was not otherwise disturbed; when the moment was past, she looked down at the fire again.

151
00:13:44,879 --> 00:13:50,559
“I cannot think,” said Estella, raising her eyes after a silence “why you should

152
00:13:50,559 --> 00:13:55,078
be so unreasonable when I come to see you after a separation. I have never

153
00:13:55,078 --> 00:14:00,398
forgotten your wrongs and their causes. I have never been unfaithful to you or

154
00:14:00,398 --> 00:14:07,160
your schooling. I have never shown any weakness that I can charge myself with.”

155
00:14:07,160 --> 00:14:13,436
“Would it be weakness to return my love?” exclaimed Miss Havisham. “But yes, yes,

156
00:14:13,438 --> 00:14:15,597
she would call it so!”

157
00:14:15,597 --> 00:14:21,277
“I begin to think,” said Estella, in a musing way, after another moment of calm wonder,

158
00:14:21,277 --> 00:14:26,597
“that I almost understand how this comes about. If you had brought up your adopted

159
00:14:26,597 --> 00:14:31,879
daughter wholly in the dark confinement of these rooms, and had never let her know that

160
00:14:31,879 --> 00:14:37,358
there was such a thing as the daylight by which she had never once seen your face,—if

161
00:14:37,358 --> 00:14:42,160
you had done that, and then, for a purpose had wanted her to understand

162
00:14:42,160 --> 00:14:48,519
the daylight and know all about it, you would have been disappointed and angry?”

163
00:14:48,519 --> 00:14:51,718
Miss Havisham, with her head in her hands, sat making a low moaning,

164
00:14:51,718 --> 00:14:56,319
and swaying herself on her chair, but gave no answer.

165
00:14:56,319 --> 00:15:01,758
“Or,” said Estella,—“which is a nearer case,—if you had taught her,

166
00:15:01,758 --> 00:15:05,438
from the dawn of her intelligence, with your utmost energy and might,

167
00:15:05,438 --> 00:15:10,398
that there was such a thing as daylight, but that it was made to be her enemy and destroyer,

168
00:15:10,398 --> 00:15:13,877
and she must always turn against it, for it had blighted you and would else blight

169
00:15:13,879 --> 00:15:20,116
her;—if you had done this, and then, for a purpose, had wanted her to take naturally to

170
00:15:20,116 --> 00:15:26,398
the daylight and she could not do it, you would have been disappointed and angry?”

171
00:15:26,398 --> 00:15:28,957
Miss Havisham sat listening (or it seemed so,

172
00:15:28,957 --> 00:15:34,078
for I could not see her face), but still made no answer.

173
00:15:34,078 --> 00:15:40,917
“So,” said Estella, “I must be taken as I have been made. The success is not mine, the failure

174
00:15:40,917 --> 00:15:48,597
is not mine, but the two together make me.” Miss Havisham had settled down, I hardly knew how,

175
00:15:48,597 --> 00:15:54,438
upon the floor, among the faded bridal relics with which it was strewn. I took

176
00:15:54,438 --> 00:16:00,160
advantage of the moment—I had sought one from the first—to leave the room, after beseeching

177
00:16:00,160 --> 00:16:06,879
Estella’s attention to her, with a movement of my hand. When I left, Estella was yet standing

178
00:16:06,879 --> 00:16:12,438
by the great chimney-piece, just as she had stood throughout. Miss Havisham’s grey hair

179
00:16:12,438 --> 00:16:19,358
was all adrift upon the ground, among the other bridal wrecks, and was a miserable sight to see.

180
00:16:19,358 --> 00:16:23,557
It was with a depressed heart that I walked in the starlight for an hour and more,

181
00:16:23,559 --> 00:16:29,438
about the courtyard, and about the brewery, and about the ruined garden. When I at last

182
00:16:29,438 --> 00:16:34,758
took courage to return to the room, I found Estella sitting at Miss Havisham’s knee,

183
00:16:34,758 --> 00:16:39,758
taking up some stitches in one of those old articles of dress that were dropping to pieces,

184
00:16:39,758 --> 00:16:45,480
and of which I have often been reminded since by the faded tatters of old banners that I have seen

185
00:16:45,480 --> 00:16:53,199
hanging up in cathedrals. Afterwards, Estella and I played at cards, as of yore,—only we

186
00:16:53,199 --> 00:17:01,718
were skilful now, and played French games,—and so the evening wore away, and I went to bed.

187
00:17:01,718 --> 00:17:06,800
I lay in that separate building across the courtyard. It was the first time I had

188
00:17:06,800 --> 00:17:13,839
ever lain down to rest in Satis House, and sleep refused to come near me. A thousand Miss Havishams

189
00:17:13,839 --> 00:17:20,519
haunted me. She was on this side of my pillow, on that, at the head of the bed, at the foot,

190
00:17:20,519 --> 00:17:25,957
behind the half-opened door of the dressing-room, in the dressing-room, in the room overhead,

191
00:17:25,957 --> 00:17:33,480
in the room beneath,—everywhere. At last, when the night was slow to creep on towards

192
00:17:33,480 --> 00:17:40,358
two o’clock, I felt that I absolutely could no longer bear the place as a place to lie down in,

193
00:17:40,358 --> 00:17:46,800
and that I must get up. I therefore got up and put on my clothes, and went out across the yard into

194
00:17:46,800 --> 00:17:52,078
the long stone passage, designing to gain the outer courtyard and walk there for the relief

195
00:17:52,078 --> 00:17:58,680
of my mind. But I was no sooner in the passage than I extinguished my candle; for I saw Miss

196
00:17:58,680 --> 00:18:05,597
Havisham going along it in a ghostly manner, making a low cry. I followed her at a distance,

197
00:18:05,597 --> 00:18:11,358
and saw her go up the staircase. She carried a bare candle in her hand, which she had probably

198
00:18:11,358 --> 00:18:18,038
taken from one of the sconces in her own room, and was a most unearthly object by its light.

199
00:18:18,038 --> 00:18:22,957
Standing at the bottom of the staircase, I felt the mildewed air of the feast-chamber,

200
00:18:22,957 --> 00:18:29,519
without seeing her open the door, and I heard her walking there, and so across into her own room,

201
00:18:29,519 --> 00:18:36,758
and so across again into that, never ceasing the low cry. After a time, I tried in the dark both

202
00:18:36,758 --> 00:18:43,839
to get out, and to go back, but I could do neither until some streaks of day strayed in and showed me

203
00:18:43,839 --> 00:18:50,438
where to lay my hands. During the whole interval, whenever I went to the bottom of the staircase,

204
00:18:50,438 --> 00:18:57,319
I heard her footstep, saw her light pass above, and heard her ceaseless low cry.

205
00:18:57,319 --> 00:19:02,917
Before we left next day, there was no revival of the difference between her and Estella,

206
00:19:02,917 --> 00:19:08,917
nor was it ever revived on any similar occasion; and there were four similar occasions, to the best

207
00:19:08,917 --> 00:19:14,641
of my remembrance. Nor, did Miss Havisham’s manner towards Estella in anywise change,

208
00:19:14,641 --> 00:19:21,078
except that I believed it to have something like fear infused among its former characteristics.

209
00:19:21,078 --> 00:19:24,436
It is impossible to turn this leaf of my life,

210
00:19:24,438 --> 00:19:31,116
without putting Bentley Drummle’s name upon it; or I would, very gladly.

211
00:19:31,116 --> 00:19:35,917
On a certain occasion when the Finches were assembled in force, and when good feeling was

212
00:19:35,917 --> 00:19:41,199
being promoted in the usual manner by nobody’s agreeing with anybody else, the presiding Finch

213
00:19:41,199 --> 00:19:46,639
called the Grove to order, forasmuch as Mr. Drummle had not yet toasted a lady; which,

214
00:19:46,641 --> 00:19:52,199
according to the solemn constitution of the society, it was the brute’s turn to do that

215
00:19:52,199 --> 00:19:58,879
day. I thought I saw him leer in an ugly way at me while the decanters were going round,

216
00:19:58,879 --> 00:20:04,559
but as there was no love lost between us, that might easily be. What was my

217
00:20:04,559 --> 00:20:09,559
indignant surprise when he called upon the company to pledge him to “Estella!”

218
00:20:09,559 --> 00:20:11,718
“Estella who?” said I.

219
00:20:11,718 --> 00:20:14,559
“Never you mind,” retorted Drummle.

220
00:20:14,559 --> 00:20:21,879
“Estella of where?” said I. “You are bound to say of where.” Which he was, as a Finch.

221
00:20:21,879 --> 00:20:29,000
“Of Richmond, gentlemen,” said Drummle, putting me out of the question, “and a peerless beauty.”

222
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:36,718
Much he knew about peerless beauties, a mean, miserable idiot! I whispered Herbert.

223
00:20:36,718 --> 00:20:42,000
“I know that lady,” said Herbert, across the table, when the toast had been honoured.

224
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:43,957
“Do you?” said Drummle.

225
00:20:43,957 --> 00:20:47,358
“And so do I,” I added, with a scarlet face.

226
00:20:47,358 --> 00:20:55,800
“Do you?” said Drummle. “O, Lord!” This was the only retort—except glass or

227
00:20:55,800 --> 00:21:00,839
crockery—that the heavy creature was capable of making; but, I became as highly incensed

228
00:21:00,839 --> 00:21:05,917
by it as if it had been barbed with wit, and I immediately rose in my place and said that

229
00:21:05,917 --> 00:21:10,718
I could not but regard it as being like the honourable Finch’s impudence to come down to

230
00:21:10,718 --> 00:21:16,160
that Grove,—we always talked about coming down to that Grove, as a neat Parliamentary turn of

231
00:21:16,160 --> 00:21:22,718
expression,—down to that Grove, proposing a lady of whom he knew nothing. Mr. Drummle,

232
00:21:22,718 --> 00:21:29,116
upon this, starting up, demanded what I meant by that? Whereupon I made him the extreme reply

233
00:21:29,116 --> 00:21:34,000
that I believed he knew where I was to be found. Whether it was possible in a Christian country

234
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:39,237
to get on without blood, after this, was a question on which the Finches were divided.

235
00:21:39,237 --> 00:21:44,758
The debate upon it grew so lively, indeed, that at least six more honourable members told six more,

236
00:21:44,758 --> 00:21:50,597
during the discussion, that they believed they knew where they were to be found. However,

237
00:21:50,597 --> 00:21:56,680
it was decided at last (the Grove being a Court of Honour) that if Mr. Drummle would bring never so

238
00:21:56,680 --> 00:22:01,758
slight a certificate from the lady, importing that he had the honour of her acquaintance,

239
00:22:01,758 --> 00:22:09,078
Mr. Pip must express his regret, as a gentleman and a Finch, for “having been betrayed into a

240
00:22:09,078 --> 00:22:15,641
warmth which.” Next day was appointed for the production (lest our honour should take

241
00:22:15,641 --> 00:22:22,000
cold from delay), and next day Drummle appeared with a polite little avowal in Estella’s hand,

242
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:27,641
that she had had the honour of dancing with him several times. This left me no course

243
00:22:27,641 --> 00:22:33,597
but to regret that I had been “betrayed into a warmth which,” and on the whole to repudiate,

244
00:22:33,597 --> 00:22:40,917
as untenable, the idea that I was to be found anywhere. Drummle and I then sat snorting at

245
00:22:40,917 --> 00:22:46,398
one another for an hour, while the Grove engaged in indiscriminate contradiction,

246
00:22:46,398 --> 00:22:53,436
and finally the promotion of good feeling was declared to have gone ahead at an amazing rate.

247
00:22:53,438 --> 00:22:59,758
I tell this lightly, but it was no light thing to me. For, I cannot adequately express what pain it

248
00:22:59,758 --> 00:23:05,800
gave me to think that Estella should show any favour to a contemptible, clumsy, sulky booby,

249
00:23:05,800 --> 00:23:12,641
so very far below the average. To the present moment, I believe it to have been referable to

250
00:23:12,641 --> 00:23:19,480
some pure fire of generosity and disinterestedness in my love for her, that I could not endure the

251
00:23:19,480 --> 00:23:24,838
thought of her stooping to that hound. No doubt I should have been miserable whomsoever she had

252
00:23:24,839 --> 00:23:31,000
favoured; but a worthier object would have caused me a different kind and degree of distress.

253
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:36,199
It was easy for me to find out, and I did soon find out, that Drummle had begun to follow her

254
00:23:36,199 --> 00:23:42,957
closely, and that she allowed him to do it. A little while, and he was always in pursuit of her,

255
00:23:42,957 --> 00:23:49,519
and he and I crossed one another every day. He held on, in a dull persistent way, and Estella

256
00:23:49,519 --> 00:23:55,957
held him on; now with encouragement, now with discouragement, now almost flattering him,

257
00:23:55,957 --> 00:24:03,879
now openly despising him, now knowing him very well, now scarcely remembering who he was.

258
00:24:03,879 --> 00:24:08,800
The Spider, as Mr. Jaggers had called him, was used to lying in wait, however,

259
00:24:08,800 --> 00:24:15,078
and had the patience of his tribe. Added to that, he had a blockhead confidence in his money and in

260
00:24:15,078 --> 00:24:20,358
his family greatness, which sometimes did him good service,—almost taking the place

261
00:24:20,358 --> 00:24:26,838
of concentration and determined purpose. So, the Spider, doggedly watching Estella,

262
00:24:26,839 --> 00:24:35,358
outwatched many brighter insects, and would often uncoil himself and drop at the right nick of time.

263
00:24:35,358 --> 00:24:40,557
At a certain Assembly Ball at Richmond (there used to be Assembly Balls at most places then),

264
00:24:40,559 --> 00:24:45,957
where Estella had outshone all other beauties, this blundering Drummle so hung about her,

265
00:24:45,957 --> 00:24:52,160
and with so much toleration on her part, that I resolved to speak to her concerning him. I took

266
00:24:52,160 --> 00:24:57,680
the next opportunity; which was when she was waiting for Mrs. Blandley to take her home,

267
00:24:57,680 --> 00:25:02,917
and was sitting apart among some flowers, ready to go. I was with her,

268
00:25:02,917 --> 00:25:06,038
for I almost always accompanied them to and from such places.

269
00:25:07,199 --> 00:25:09,639
“Are you tired, Estella?”

270
00:25:09,641 --> 00:25:11,237
“Rather, Pip.”

271
00:25:11,237 --> 00:25:12,839
“You should be.”

272
00:25:12,839 --> 00:25:18,559
“Say rather, I should not be; for I have my letter to Satis House to write, before I go to sleep.”

273
00:25:18,559 --> 00:25:24,000
“Recounting to-night’s triumph?” said I. “Surely a very poor one, Estella.”

274
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:27,319
“What do you mean? I didn’t know there had been any.”

275
00:25:27,319 --> 00:25:33,758
“Estella,” said I, “do look at that fellow in the corner yonder, who is looking over here at us.”

276
00:25:33,758 --> 00:25:38,879
“Why should I look at him?” returned Estella, with her eyes on me instead. “What is there

277
00:25:38,879 --> 00:25:44,597
in that fellow in the corner yonder,—to use your words,—that I need look at?”

278
00:25:44,597 --> 00:25:47,398
“Indeed, that is the very question I want to ask

279
00:25:47,398 --> 00:25:52,358
you,” said I. “For he has been hovering about you all night.”

280
00:25:52,358 --> 00:25:56,319
“Moths, and all sorts of ugly creatures,” replied Estella,

281
00:25:56,319 --> 00:26:02,957
with a glance towards him, “hover about a lighted candle. Can the candle help it?”

282
00:26:02,957 --> 00:26:07,480
“No,” I returned; “but cannot the Estella help it?”

283
00:26:07,480 --> 00:26:13,838
“Well!” said she, laughing, after a moment, “perhaps. Yes. Anything you like.”

284
00:26:13,839 --> 00:26:17,438
“But, Estella, do hear me speak. It makes me wretched that you should

285
00:26:17,438 --> 00:26:22,917
encourage a man so generally despised as Drummle. You know he is despised.”

286
00:26:22,917 --> 00:26:29,718
“Well?” said she. “You know he is as ungainly within as without. A

287
00:26:29,718 --> 00:26:36,160
deficient, ill-tempered, lowering, stupid fellow.” “Well?” said she.

288
00:26:36,160 --> 00:26:38,557
“You know he has nothing to recommend him but money and a

289
00:26:38,559 --> 00:26:43,116
ridiculous roll of addle-headed predecessors; now, don’t you?”

290
00:26:43,116 --> 00:26:49,680
“Well?” said she again; and each time she said it, she opened her lovely eyes the wider.

291
00:26:49,680 --> 00:26:54,237
To overcome the difficulty of getting past that monosyllable, I took it from her,

292
00:26:54,237 --> 00:27:01,718
and said, repeating it with emphasis, “Well! Then, that is why it makes me wretched.”

293
00:27:01,718 --> 00:27:07,839
Now, if I could have believed that she favoured Drummle with any idea of making me—me—wretched,

294
00:27:08,839 --> 00:27:13,718
I should have been in better heart about it; but in that habitual way of hers,

295
00:27:13,718 --> 00:27:19,800
she put me so entirely out of the question, that I could believe nothing of the kind.

296
00:27:19,800 --> 00:27:26,277
“Pip,” said Estella, casting her glance over the room, “don’t be foolish about its effect on

297
00:27:26,277 --> 00:27:32,800
you. It may have its effect on others, and may be meant to have. It’s not worth discussing.”

298
00:27:32,800 --> 00:27:37,116
“Yes it is,” said I, “because I cannot bear that people should say,

299
00:27:37,116 --> 00:27:43,319
‘she throws away her graces and attractions on a mere boor, the lowest in the crowd.’”

300
00:27:43,319 --> 00:27:45,917
“I can bear it,” said Estella.

301
00:27:45,917 --> 00:27:50,319
“Oh! don’t be so proud, Estella, and so inflexible.”

302
00:27:50,319 --> 00:27:54,078
“Calls me proud and inflexible in this breath!” said Estella,

303
00:27:54,078 --> 00:28:00,557
opening her hands. “And in his last breath reproached me for stooping to a boor!”

304
00:28:00,559 --> 00:28:04,116
“There is no doubt you do,” said I, something hurriedly,

305
00:28:04,116 --> 00:28:11,358
“for I have seen you give him looks and smiles this very night, such as you never give to—me.”

306
00:28:11,358 --> 00:28:17,160
“Do you want me then,” said Estella, turning suddenly with a fixed and serious,

307
00:28:17,160 --> 00:28:20,957
if not angry, look, “to deceive and entrap you?”

308
00:28:20,957 --> 00:28:24,000
“Do you deceive and entrap him, Estella?”

309
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:31,038
“Yes, and many others,—all of them but you. Here is Mrs. Brandley. I’ll say no more.”

310
00:28:31,038 --> 00:28:35,436
And now that I have given the one chapter to the theme that so filled my heart,

311
00:28:35,438 --> 00:28:40,519
and so often made it ache and ache again, I pass on unhindered,

312
00:28:40,519 --> 00:28:46,680
to the event that had impended over me longer yet; the event that had begun to be prepared for,

313
00:28:46,680 --> 00:28:52,000
before I knew that the world held Estella, and in the days when her baby intelligence

314
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:58,116
was receiving its first distortions from Miss Havisham’s wasting hands.

315
00:28:58,116 --> 00:29:04,436
In the Eastern story, the heavy slab that was to fall on the bed of state in the flush of conquest

316
00:29:04,438 --> 00:29:09,957
was slowly wrought out of the quarry, the tunnel for the rope to hold it in its place was slowly

317
00:29:09,957 --> 00:29:16,000
carried through the leagues of rock, the slab was slowly raised and fitted in the roof, the

318
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:22,758
rope was rove to it and slowly taken through the miles of hollow to the great iron ring. All being

319
00:29:22,758 --> 00:29:29,358
made ready with much labour, and the hour come, the sultan was aroused in the dead of the night,

320
00:29:29,358 --> 00:29:35,358
and the sharpened axe that was to sever the rope from the great iron ring was put into his hand,

321
00:29:35,358 --> 00:29:41,879
and he struck with it, and the rope parted and rushed away, and the ceiling fell. So,

322
00:29:41,879 --> 00:29:48,758
in my case; all the work, near and afar, that tended to the end, had been accomplished;

323
00:29:48,758 --> 00:29:54,000
and in an instant the blow was struck, and the roof of my stronghold dropped upon me.
